    "code":  "ADVERSE_DRUG_REACTION_ELDERLY",
    "name":  "Adverse drug reaction in elderly",
    "module":  "geriatric",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Adverse drug reactions (ADRs) in elderly patients are disproportionately common due to altered pharmacokinetics (reduced renal clearance, decreased hepatic metabolism, reduced volume of distribution for water-soluble drugs, increased fat distribution for lipophilic drugs) and pharmacodynamics (increased CNS sensitivity to sedatives, reduced baroreflex response to antihypertensives). The most clinically significant ADRs in older adults include falls from sedatives or antihypertensives, delirium from anticholinergics or opioids, bleeding from anticoagulants, hypoglycaemia from hypoglycaemics, and renal impairment from NSAIDs or ACE inhibitors. The Beers Criteria and STOPP/START tools provide evidence-based guidance on inappropriate prescribing in older adults.",
